<Project>

  <!--
    THE version. Every other version value in the repo derives from this one, so a release is a
    one-character edit here — and the derivations now live HERE too, next to the number, rather than being
    restated in each csproj. They used to be restated, and the restatements drifted:

      Console.Lib          AssemblyVersion  stuck at 3.6.0.0 from 4.1 through 4.8
      MdCat                VersionPrefix    read 3.15.0 while the library was on 4.8
      Console.Lib.Inspector VersionPrefix   read 1.0.0 for its entire life

    It is the X.Y only, because that is the part every package shares; the patch/build component differs
    by consumer (local and debug builds use .0, CI substitutes its run number).

    AssemblyVersion is derived on purpose, and unconditionally. It is the one value CI does NOT stamp — the
    workflow passes -p:Version and -p:FileVersion but never -p:AssemblyVersion — so a per-project literal
    wins in exactly the build that ships. That is how DIR.Lib published 6.4.0.0 for two majors and
    SdlVulkan.Renderer published 6.11.0.0; a stale VersionPrefix only spoils a local pack, a stale
    AssemblyVersion spoils the package. Only Major.Minor is significant: the build counter stays out, so a
    republish of the same X.Y does not change assembly identity.

    Every package here is version-melded to the library, INCLUDING Console.Lib.Inspector. Its csproj used to
    carry VersionPrefix 1.0.0 under the intent that it was separately versioned, but CI's solution-wide
    -p:Version overrode that on every run, so nuget.org has only ever had it in lockstep (4.16.1511 today,
    and 4.3.1341 as its first release — never a 1.0.0). The literal therefore did not deliver separate
    versioning; it just made local packs disagree with published ones. If it should genuinely diverge, it
    needs its own VersionMajorMinor and its own pack step, not a literal CI silently ignores.

    CI derives it too, so this really is the only place. .github/workflows/dotnet.yml no longer carries a
    static VERSION_PREFIX: a workflow env: block cannot evaluate MSBuild, so the build job resolves the
    number in a step (`dotnet msbuild -getProperty:VersionMajorMinor` into $GITHUB_ENV) before it stamps
    -p:Version across the solution. CI therefore cannot publish a version the packages disagree with. The
    release-note block stays in that env: several entries contain a double hyphen, which XML forbids
    inside a comment, so the notes cannot move next to the number they describe — add the entry there
    when you bump this.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <VersionMajorMinor>4.26</VersionMajorMinor>
    <!-- Local builds get Major.Minor.0; CI passes -p:Version=Major.Minor.<run><attempt>+<sha>, and the
         condition lets it win. -->
    <VersionPrefix Condition="'$(VersionPrefix)' == ''">$(VersionMajorMinor).0</VersionPrefix>
    <AssemblyVersion>$(VersionMajorMinor).0.0</AssemblyVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
